covered fills: 710,129 | niche fills: 43,080 (218 contracts with any pre-start tape of 962 priced)

=== taker P&L to settlement: MM-present vs MM-absent ===
                   class    fills    $ staked   gross  net fee  cl.se
  covered games (MM $817K)  710,129  95,734,645  -5.0%   -7.7%   7.0%
   niche games (MM ~$6K)   43,080   1,845,382  -7.1%  -10.0%  10.5%

  median fill size: covered 30 vs niche 26 contracts
  niche fills per contract: median 70 (vs ~1,384 per covered game)

=== reading ===
  The class actions' implicit prediction — customers fare better
  away from the house's book — finds no support: takers lost 5.0%
  gross (7.7% net) where the MM complex stands and 7.1% (10.0%
  net) where it does not; the difference is inside the cluster
  SEs, so the supportable claim is 'no better, possibly worse.'
  The first-order effect of the house's absence is QUANTITY:
  only 218 of 962 niche contracts saw any pre-start fill at all.
  The house's presence is what makes there be a price; what it
  costs is the same ~5% a sportsbook charges. Whether that is
  'betting the house' or 'liquidity provision' is the question
  the CFTC's 2026-07-30 bona fide MM proposal exists to answer.
